ld-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294733}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "771eaa44-b3e5-5365-b2c4-cde4e6dfbce5" :question/math-academy-id "q-276950" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\operatorname{log}⁡(2-x)+1\\le \\operatorname{log}⁡(x+9).$\n\n{{field-1}}$\\le \\,x$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}" :question/worked-solution "We use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $10.$ This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{10}^{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2-x)+1} & \\le {10}^{\\operatorname{log}⁡(x+9)} \\\\ {10}^{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2-x)}\\cdot {10}^{1} & \\le {10}^{\\operatorname{log}⁡(x+9)} \\\\ (2-x)\\cdot 10 & \\le x+9 \\\\ 20-10x & \\le x+9 \\\\ -11x & \\le -11 \\\\ x & \\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithms in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithms, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-x & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & <2 \\\\ x+9 & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x\\ge 1,$ which can be expressed as $[1,\\infty ).$ Additionally, the domains of the logarithms are $x<2$ and $x>-11,$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty ,2)$ and $(-11,\\infty )$ respectively. To find the solutions that are in the domains of the logarithms, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n[1,\\infty )\\cap (-\\infty ,2)\\cap (-9,\\infty )=[1,2)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in [1,2),$ which can also be written as $1\\le x<2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157906 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157326 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "106dce79-251c-52b2-adc7-9fd2b59b9b83" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving Two Logarithmic Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "280096e0-cde8-5384-abed-9889ee8c3d70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{2}{3{e}^{5}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351936} {:answer/id #uuid "3b4156e5-c4bd-5af4-a9e3-c3a097fd3a5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3{e}^{5}-1}\\le x\\le 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352112} {:answer/id #uuid "49d9d950-8ac2-5fd8-ac24-12026629f145" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<x\\le \\frac{2}{3{e}^{5}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352245} {:answer/id #uuid "b15ac860-a9b2-55a4-a137-b703da796b6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{2}{3{e}^{5}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353084} {:answer/id #uuid "d2f8dc3a-d368-56ca-81d8-7f244b50521e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3{e}^{5}-1}\\le x\\le \\frac{2}{3{e}^{5}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353393}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49d9d950-8ac2-5fd8-ac24-12026629f145" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<x\\le \\frac{2}{3{e}^{5}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352245} :answer-field/id #uuid "defcf317-4b7e-5823-9a04-19d2dd5bd1d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354197}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c25ae1f2-3afc-5e1a-9d69-5f676e0bbd4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-87745" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)-5\\ge \\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x).$" :question/worked-solution "We use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $e.$ This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)-5} & \\ge {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)} \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)}\\cdot {e}^{-5} & \\ge {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)} \\\\ (x+2){e}^{-5} & \\ge 3x \\\\ x+2 & \\ge 3{e}^{5}x \\\\ 2 & \\ge (3{e}^{5}-1)x \\\\ \\frac{2}{3{e}^{5}-1} & \\ge x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithms in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithms, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2 & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >-2 \\\\ 3x & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x\\le \\frac{2}{3{e}^{5}-1},$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty ,\\frac{2}{3{e}^{5}-1}].$ Additionally, the domains of the logarithms are $x>-2$ and $x>0,$ which can be expressed as $(-2,\\infty )$ and $(0,\\infty )$ respectively. To find the solutions that are in the domains of the logarithms, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,\\frac{2}{3{e}^{5}-1}]\\cap (-2,\\infty )\\cap (0,\\infty )=(0,\\frac{2}{3{e}^{5}-1}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in (0,\\frac{2}{3{e}^{5}-1}],$ which can also be written as $0<x\\le \\frac{2}{3{e}^{5}-1}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354621 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157325 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d522133e-f7ca-5cbb-8a42-8377a685b263" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving One Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c15e74b-fed6-536b-8944-a40c16b27137" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{1+{e}^{6}}{2{e}^{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351820} {:answer/id #uuid "2f59e3ed-daf9-55b2-8a90-4c883aa8642b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2<x\\le \\frac{1+2{e}^{6}}{{e}^{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352003} {:answer/id #uuid "7faa8f0b-6d24-5e0a-8116-3f4792d5f868" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{2+2{e}^{6}}{{e}^{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352685} {:answer/id #uuid "a04004f7-30ef-5c0e-96e1-45fc8d660c55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{1-2{e}^{6}}{{e}^{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352939} {:answer/id #uuid "fc10efd9-2ded-5508-b1db-15bc3cf91cc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{1+2{e}^{6}}{{e}^{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353773}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc10efd9-2ded-5508-b1db-15bc3cf91cc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{1+2{e}^{6}}{{e}^{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353773} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa7e0d19-a1f8-5f96-a52c-2bc8fe4cf729" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354251}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "03f5c85f-7df7-57a2-a308-f1fc35e5399c" :question/math-academy-id "q-88006" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)+4\\ge 0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the logarithm:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)+4 & \\ge 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2) & \\ge -4 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2) & \\ge -6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $e.$ We get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)} & \\ge {e}^{-6} \\\\ x-2 & \\ge {e}^{-6} \\\\ x & \\ge {e}^{-6}+2 \\\\ x & \\ge \\frac{1+2{e}^{6}}{{e}^{6}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithm, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\nx-2>0\\,⇒\\,x>2\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x\\ge \\frac{1+2{e}^{6}}{{e}^{6}},$ which can be expressed as $[\\frac{1+2{e}^{6}}{{e}^{6}},\\infty ).$ Additionally, the domain of the logarithm is $x>2,$ which can be expressed as $(2,\\infty ).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n[\\frac{1+2{e}^{6}}{{e}^{6}},\\infty )\\cap (2,\\infty )=[\\frac{1+2{e}^{6}}{{e}^{6}},\\infty )\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in [\\frac{1+2{e}^{6}}{{e}^{6}},\\infty ),$ which can also be written as $x\\ge \\frac{1+2{e}^{6}}{{e}^{6}}.$"}]}
